338 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Office Troll is a satirical political simulation game developed in Unity 2022 LTS, structured around continuous management of a persistent game state in line with established simulation game design conventions \(Novak, 2012\). Following the 2D-to-3D revision discussed in Phase 1, the final development build combines a third-person, walkable 3D office environment with 2D-style UI panels that overlay the scene for the core decision-making gameplay. The game is set within the South African Department of Home Affairs and places the player in the role of a senior document processing clerk who is simultaneously accountable to the public and pressured by a corrupt patronage network. The development phase involved the implementation of the 3D office and player-character systems, all core decision-making mechanics, UI systems, audio, and event content. The codebase is built around an event-driven architecture using Unity ScriptableObjects for game data and C# Action delegates for inter-system communication, consistent with the object-oriented design principles specified in the brief \(Nystrom, 2014\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
339 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (OBJECT-ORIENTED DESIGN AND ARCHITECTURE)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
340 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 103 0 R
/T (Single Responsibility Principle)
/E (Single Responsibility Principle)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
341 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (Each C# script in the project handles exactly one concern. WorldState.cs tracks all runtime game variables. EventManager.cs selects the appropriate event each turn. GameManager.cs coordinates the turn loop without performing any of the work itself. BribeListManager.cs manages the bribe system in isolation. No script performs more than one type of work, following the single responsibility principle as described by Martin \(2017, p. 138\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
342 0 obj
<<
